{
  "summary": "The authors report a cross-sectional 'natural experiment' (N = 1,144 Australian, New Zealand and US Prolific gamers) comparing self-isolated or quarantined participants with non-isolated participants. They find no higher distress-independent gaming, loot box spending or risky loot box use in the isolated group, but a stronger correlation between PGSI scores and loot box spending (rs = .361 vs .221). Contamination concern is weakly to moderately associated with gaming and loot box measures. They conclude that pandemic isolation may inflate effect sizes in gaming and addiction research, that more problem gamblers may be buying loot boxes, and that these effects may be temporary coping responses. The design is observational, the isolation groups were not randomised, and several headline claims go beyond the tests reported.",
  "issues": [
    {
      "claim": "The isolated versus non-isolated comparison is presented as a natural experiment that isolates the effect of pandemic isolation, but group membership was not assigned and no baseline or covariate adjustment is reported.",
      "issue_type": "V2",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "There was no theoretical reason to believe that the pandemic had selectively isolated gamers or",
          "location_hint": "Methods, Participants, lines 128-129"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 9,
          "observation": "Lines 126-131 describe the design as a natural experiment and state that there was no theoretical reason to think isolation selected gamers or problem gamblers. No evidence, covariates or balance checks are shown on this page."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Self-isolation and quarantine were not randomly assigned, so the groups may differ in age, employment, household, country, prior distress or lockdown exposure. The 'no theoretical reason' argument is an assertion, not a test. Without covariate-adjusted models or group-balance data, the reported group differences and the moderation result cannot be read as effects of isolation. This weakens the central causal framing in the abstract and discussion."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The pre-registered moderation hypothesis (H4) that isolation moderates the PGSI category effect on loot box spending is reported only as a null verbal statement, with no test statistic.",
      "issue_type": "V3",
      "status": "missing_information",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "self-isolation or quarantine did not moderate this relationship",
          "location_hint": "Results, lines 214-216"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 20,
          "observation": "The Figure 1 caption reports a main effect of PGSI group, F(3, 1,131) = 34.679, and planned pairwise comparisons only. No isolation-by-PGSI interaction statistic is shown here or elsewhere in the main text."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "Hypothesis 4 is one of the pre-registered tests. The manuscript gives no interaction F, coefficient, or p-value for it, so readers cannot check whether the null is a formal non-significant interaction or simply an impression from the bar chart. The conclusion that isolation strengthens the association is drawn from a continuous correlation comparison. The categorical moderation result is not reconciled with that conclusion. This affects the claim that the PGSI-spending association is stronger under isolation."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The discussion and abstract infer changes in the prevalence of problem gamblers buying loot boxes and a general inflation of effect sizes in gaming research, neither of which the analyses test.",
      "issue_type": "V8",
      "status": "suggestion",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "more problem gamblers may be purchasing loot boxes during the pandemic",
          "location_hint": "Abstract, lines 42-43"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "may inflate certain effect sizes of relationships in",
          "location_hint": "Abstract, lines 46-47"
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The study has no pre-pandemic baseline and no comparison of the proportion of problem gamblers who buy loot boxes between groups. The discussion acknowledges that average spending by PGSI category did not differ, and then infers an increase in the number of problem gamblers purchasing loot boxes. This inference is not supported by the data. The claim that isolation inflates effect sizes in the wider literature is also not tested: it rests on one within-sample difference of correlations (Z = 2.523) and no comparison with pre-pandemic estimates. The conclusions should be limited to what the cross-sectional comparisons show."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Contamination concern, measured with the Padua Inventory contamination subscale, is interpreted as a measure of pandemic-related germ concern used to cope with the pandemic.",
      "issue_type": "V1",
      "status": "suggestion",
      "severity": "major",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "The Padua-Inventory 10 is a psychiatric scale assessing obsessive compulsive symptomology",
          "location_hint": "Measures, Contamination Concern, lines 175-176"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "their contamination concerns about the Covid-19 pandemic",
          "location_hint": "Discussion, lines 229-230"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 11,
          "observation": "Lines 174-180 describe the contamination subscale as an obsessive-compulsive symptom scale with general items such as touching objects touched by strangers and feeling hands dirty when touching money. None of the example items refers to the pandemic or to Covid-19."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The subscale measures a general obsessive-compulsive disposition, not concern about Covid-19. The discussion's coping interpretation, that people game to manage pandemic-related contamination worries, therefore rests on a construct the instrument does not measure. The association with gaming and loot box measures could reflect trait contamination concern. The coping conclusion should be treated as untested unless a pandemic-specific measure was used."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The sample size for the isolated group is reported inconsistently in the abstract and the procedure.",
      "issue_type": "V7",
      "status": "confirmed_error",
      "severity": "minor",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "(n = 447) or not (n = 619)",
          "location_hint": "Abstract, line 31"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "manuscript_text",
          "exact_quote": "or neither (n = 697)",
          "location_hint": "Procedure, lines 187-188"
        },
        {
          "input_field": "page_images",
          "page": 11,
          "observation": "Lines 187-188 report self-isolation (n = 250), quarantine (n = 197), or neither (n = 697). This gives 447 isolated and 697 non-isolated, which sums to 1,144. The abstract's n = 619 for non-isolated participants conflicts with this."
        }
      ],
      "reasoning": "The abstract gives 619 non-isolated participants, but the procedure gives 697, and 447 + 619 does not equal the total of 1,144. The procedure figures are internally consistent, so the abstract is most likely wrong. The error does not change the main results, but it affects the description of the natural groups that the design depends on."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "The manuscript is a clearly described pre-registered observational study with mostly readable analyses. Its main weakness is interpretive: the isolation groups were not assigned, the design lacks a pre-pandemic baseline or covariate adjustment, and several conclusions about coping, problem-gambler prevalence and inflated effect sizes go beyond the tests reported. The contamination measure is not pandemic-specific. The reported moderation hypothesis lacks a test statistic, and the continuous and categorical results point in different directions. Reporting has small errors: the abstract's non-isolated n conflicts with the procedure, and in Table 1 the IGD z-test p-value (.426) does not match Z = 1.028, which gives roughly p = .30. These do not overturn the null isolation findings, but the causal and effect-size claims should be substantially tempered. Supplementary materials were not supplied, so lockdown analyses could not be checked."
}
